# ── Everything goes through the API gateway ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Deployed: vercel.json rewrites the relative paths this app calls to the
# gateway, which authenticates, meters and routes them to the real service.
# The browser only ever talks to this app's own origin, so CORS never applies.
#
#     https://api-gateway-two-nu.vercel.app
#
# That is why the VITE_* base URLs below stay EMPTY. Setting one bakes an
# absolute URL into the bundle, so the browser calls that host directly and
# bypasses the gateway entirely — no auth enforcement, no metering, no budgets,
# and the target service then needs this app's origin in its CORS allowlist.
# Leave them unset unless you are deliberately bypassing the gateway.
#
# Local dev: the Vite proxy in vite.config.ts forwards the same paths to a
# local gateway on :8000, applying the same /v1 -> /api/knowledge/v1 rewrite
# vercel.json applies. So dev and production exercise the SAME path — a proxy
# pointed straight at knowledge-service would skip authentication, rate limits,
# budgets and metering, and hide bugs in all four until deploy.
#
#   GATEWAY_PROXY_TARGET=https://api-gateway-two-nu.vercel.app
#
# points the dev server at a deployed gateway instead. auth-service is never
# addressed directly by this app; the gateway is the only thing that talks
# to it.

# Base URL of a deployed knowledge-service instance.
# Leave unset in local dev — the Vite proxy forwards /v1/* to the gateway
# (see vite.config.ts).
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# There is no API-key variable any more. VITE_API_KEY used to send a static
# X-API-Key on every request; knowledge-service no longer accepts one, and
# while it did it OVERRODE the signed-in user's token — so the service trusted
# whatever account_id this app put in the request body instead of the token's
# verified claims, silently defeating tenant isolation.
#
# The signed-in user's JWT is now the only credential (see api/client.ts). The
# stats card (GET /v1/stats) needs a user whose token carries role="admin",
# which auth-service mints for members of the admin app account.

# Base URL of a deployed auth-service instance (sign-in/sign-up for this UI).
# Leave unset in local dev — the Vite proxy forwards /auth/* to the gateway,
# which routes it to auth-service (see vite.config.ts).
# NOTE: this token is ALSO sent as a Bearer header on every knowledge-service
# request (see api/client.ts) — for it to be accepted there, and for
# account_id/user_id to be derived from it rather than the request body, the
# target knowledge-service must have RAG_AUTH_ENABLED=true with
# RAG_JWT_SECRET/RAG_JWT_ISSUER matching this auth-service's
# AUTH_JWT_SECRET/AUTH_JWT_ISSUER.
VITE_AUTH_BASE_URL=

# The target auth-service must also set AUTHSVC_CORS_ORIGINS to include this
# app's origin (e.g. http://localhost:5174) — same default-closed CORS
# policy as knowledge-service.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── guest sign-ups
# The app account that this console's "Create User" button registers new users
# into (and the one its password resets are scoped to). REQUIRED — there is no
# built-in default and the app refuses to boot without it (src/api/authTypes.ts):
# auth-service mints account IDs at registration, so the value differs per
# deployment. Register the account through the account-management console and
# paste its account_id here. Inlined at BUILD time: on Vercel it has to be in
# the project's environment, not just this file.
VITE_GUEST_ACCOUNT_ID=
